A||N||10||terryk||Mogg K6357 Caster Shim Kit question||||5||
Z||000000||terryk||01-05-2001||09:02 PM||||Has anyone used the Moog K6357 Caster Shim kit?<P>K6357 [Shaft Kit] w/Caster Shim; Frt.; Problem Solver; Upper Control Arm Kit w/Bushings; 2 Req.  <P>1. Does it come with new control arm bushings?<BR>2. Is so, are they any different than the normal replacement bushing K6170. <BR>3. What is in the kit?<P>I'm at 1.2/1.9 degrees caster and it wanders a bit at straight-line (center).<P>I noticed that on one side, the center sleeve on the bushing extends past the rubber. On the other side, it doesn't. Either way, I need to drag the caster back nearly 5 degrees.<P>Mogg?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-05-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||01-05-2001||09:34 PM||||TerryK:<P>I have been fighting the same problem on my 87 GT.  I had 3.9 deg on the left but only 1.4 deg on the right.  The car wandered all over the road and pulled significantly to the right.  The suspension was compltely rebuilt and the alignment shop failed to tell me about the out of spec condition.  <P>The nominal spec is 5 deg with a tolerance of +- 2 deg.  <P>Be sure your alignment values are due to the control arm itself and not slop caused by worn out bushings!<P>The Moog caster adjustment set includes a new pivot bolt, new control arm bushings, and the washer set that you place on either side of the frame to position the control arm relative to the lower control arm.  What you will need to do is put all but one washer to the rear to move the control arm back to approach the factory specs.  <P>You might not need to buy the full set.  The cars normally came with 2 6mm spacer washers that fit between the frame and control arm, one in front and one behind.  GM used to sell an adjustment washer set that consisted of a 3mm and 9mm washer set that was used to replace the original 6mm spacers.  Using this set was supposed to change the caster by about 1 deg.  Not enough for your application or mine on the right side.  <P>I would first replace the control arm bushings in both upper control arms.  If you can see any space bwtwwen the bushing and frame or bushings and spacer washers then the bushing is shot and should be repalced.  Fortunately, upper control arm removeal and repalcement is pretty easy.  <P>What I did was to find a thin stainless steel wahser to put between the front bushing and the frame.  I them put BOTH 6mm washers behind the frame at the rear gap.  This should move the control arm back almost 2 deg. if my info is correct.  The thin stainless washer is needed to protect the bushing frm getting chewed up on the frame tube.  I wanted a thin one since any thickness reduces the control arm positioning that I needed.<P>I think replacing the control arm bushings and moving the washers should make the car drive much better.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-05-2001||10:04 PM||||Hi Bob,<P>Great information. I replaced all of the bushings in the car last year (Moog). Front and rear although I have Herb Adams sphericals in the rear arms. New rack and swaybar rubber. No poly.  <P>I noticed the center bushing on two top front being extended beyond the rubber. I don't know why I ignored it. Yep, my 86 has the dual 6mm washers. What's interesting is that the extended center tube only affected the caster .7 degrees. The bushing is good, but the center tube is offset in both. Strange.<P>I finally found one alignment shop that realized how easy it is to support the lower control arm and remove the upper bolt. But they didn't have the caster kit, so I figured I'd get it and go back.<P>Ok, so it sounds like I should just by two kits, install the bushing (or at least replace the strange one's) and see if the washers will push it back far enough.<P>The car wanders pretty bad when driving straight. I know more caster should correct that.<P>So did the kit correct the caster and the wandering?<P>Thanks again!<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000003||Bob Englert||01-05-2001||10:27 PM||||On my GT I didn't use the kit, I just moved BOTH 6 mm washers to the rear and used a thin stainless steel washer on the front to protect the bushing.  I really think this is what you should do on your car as well.  You don't need the fine adjustment made possible by the kit, you need to shove the control arm back as far as it will go unless the bushings you describe are bad and causing the problem.<P>I did use the kit when I rebuilt my daughter's 87 coupe.  I did it primarily to have the adjustment in case I needed it down the road.  Funny, though, I don't have any idea what it is set to, since the alignment shop I used after getting everthing together didn't give me a printout.<P>My GT did drive much better after I moved both spacers to the rear.  It is still less than 5 deg however, so I want to see if the adjustable upper ball joints might have some additional play that I can use to move the ball joint back a little more to increase the caster further.  I then need to decide if I want to put the 3/9 set on the left side, which should get that side very close to 5 deg.  I am concerned that the difference between left and right not be too great.<P>Let me know how things turn out on your car.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||01-06-2001||03:13 AM||||You know, after some math I think you are dead right. Slam them back all the way. I'll try it and see where they fall. It came up to 6.2 degrees. I'm going to replace the odd bushings tomorrow and switch the washers.<P>Thanks again!<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||01-06-2001||10:14 AM||||Bob,  I think I am going to have to take a look at my own settings.  Isn't it curious that I am also getting a pull to the right?<P>I'm planning to work on the car tommorow, so I will let you know.<P>hoop||||reg||3
Z||000006||Bob Englert||01-06-2001||10:50 AM||||TerryK:<P>I would suggest that you find a thin washer to use on the front if you put both 6mm washers to the rear.  I was concerned that the front bushing might not last long if it is direct contact with the frame tube.<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||01-06-2001||11:40 AM||||No problem Bob, I'll do that. I have some that will work fine up front.<P>I'm curious if *anyone* has a caster reading greater than 7 degrees on an 84-87. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-06-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000008||terryk||01-06-2001||12:26 PM||||bHooper,<P>Make sure it's not a tire problem. Swap them left to right and see if the pull changes. I had a terrible set of tires on the 86SE. That way you can rule this out first.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||01-06-2001||06:54 PM||||terry,<P>i know that there wasn't a pull b4 i reguilt the suspension.  but the tires are old.<P>i will be replacing the brake lines, tomorrow, so i will give everything a good inspection.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||01-07-2001||03:45 PM||||I moved both washers to the back. The wandering is gone. The steering is very solid and the straight line wandering is gone. I'm curious to see where it move the caster to. I figure about 6 degrees using my Zero-M protractor and measuring devices. <P>Thanks for your help and input.<P>ps, I replaced the upper bushings too. On one side, the center tube was clearly the wrong one (too long). All were Moog 6170. Nice that the bushings can be replaced in 30 minutes.<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-07-2001).]||||reg||14
